    {
      "id": "grammar-personal-a",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "a + persona o expresión que se refiere a una persona",
      "questionEn": "Why does Spanish sometimes put a before the person affected by an action?",
      "answerEn": "Put a before a person—or a word such as alguien or nadie—when that person directly receives the action.",
      "noteEn": "This a is called the personal a. It is common before a named or specific person, but person-denoting words such as alguien (“someone”) and nadie (“nobody”) also take it. English usually has no separate word for it. Before the masculine article el, a + el contracts to al.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Conozco a Marta.",
        "en": "I know Marta."
      },
      "tags": [
        "A2",
        "grammar",
        "personal a"
      ],
